        <section class="report-section" id="abstract">
            <p class="eyebrow">PROJECT OVERVIEW</p>
            <h2>Abstract</h2>
            <p>Hospitals coordinate many connected activities, including patient registration, appointments, clinical documentation and billing. When these activities rely on paper files or disconnected tools, staff may spend time searching for information, repeating data entry and reconciling inconsistent records. These delays can make administration harder and can affect the timely availability of information for care.</p>
            <p>This project presents the analysis and design of a Hospital Management System (HMS), a role-based web application intended to organize selected administrative and clinical workflows in one system. The proposed system covers patient and doctor profiles, appointment scheduling, medical records and billing. It describes the system requirements, architecture, data model, user interfaces, implementation approach and a test plan. Privacy, access control, data quality, usability and reliable record keeping are treated as important design requirements.</p>
            <p>The website now includes a PostgreSQL-backed implementation of staff sign-in, patient and doctor directories, appointment scheduling, consultation records and invoice/payment tracking. Its schema must be applied to the configured database and an administrator account created before these workflows can be used. The application is an academic project, not a certified clinical system; it must not be used with real patient information until the institution has approved and independently reviewed the deployment.</p>
            <div class="callout"><strong>Project boundary</strong><span>All names and examples in this report are illustrative. Do not enter real patient information into an unapproved student application.</span></div>
