        <section class="chapter-banner" id="chapter-one"><span>CHAPTER ONE</span><h2>Introduction</h2><p>Project context, purpose, objectives and boundaries.</p></section>
        <section class="report-section" id="background">
            <h3>1.1 Background of the Project</h3>
            <p>Hospitals depend on accurate and timely information to coordinate care and administration. A patient's visit may involve registration, consultation, diagnostic or treatment notes, a follow-up appointment and a bill. Each step creates or uses information. If that information is kept in separate paper files or unrelated applications, staff must bridge the gaps manually.</p>
            <p>Information systems can support this work by giving authorized staff a consistent way to record and retrieve operational information. A well-designed system does not replace professional judgement or hospital policy. Instead, it supports routine administration, provides structured records and helps staff find information relevant to their role. This project studies a web-based HMS with a deliberately defined scope that is suitable for an IT student project.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="introduction">
            <h3>1.2 Introduction</h3>
            <p>The proposed HMS is a browser-based application for managing selected hospital workflows from a shared interface. Reception staff can register patients and arrange appointments; doctors can review their schedules and document consultations; billing staff can prepare invoices; and administrators can manage staff access and reference data. Access to sensitive information is restricted by role and recorded for accountability.</p>
            <p>The project follows a structured development approach: understand the current process, define requirements, design data and interactions, implement the application, and test it against expected behaviour. This report documents those stages and provides diagrams and tables to communicate the design.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="problem-statement">
            <h3>1.3 Problem Statement</h3>
            <p>In a manual or fragmented workflow, patient details, appointment schedules, consultation notes and charges may be stored in different places. Staff may have difficulty locating the latest record, confirming whether a time slot is available or determining whether an invoice has been paid. Re-entering the same information creates opportunities for mistakes, while paper records can be misplaced or viewed by people without a work-related need.</p>
            <p>The project addresses the need for a coordinated, access-controlled system that organizes these workflows, reduces avoidable duplication and makes authorized information easier to retrieve. It does not claim that digitization alone will resolve staffing, policy, clinical or infrastructure challenges.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="aim">
            <h3>1.4 Aim of the Project</h3>
            <p>To design and document a secure, usable web-based Hospital Management System that supports patient registration, doctor administration, appointment scheduling, medical-record management and billing within a clearly defined student-project scope.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="general-objectives">
            <h3>1.5 General Objectives</h3>
            <p>The general objective is to apply systems analysis and software engineering principles to a hospital information-management problem and produce a coherent design for a maintainable, role-based application.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="specific-objectives">
            <h3>1.6 Specific Objectives</h3>
            <ol class="numbered-list">
                <li>Study the information-handling problems associated with paper-based or disconnected workflows.</li>
                <li>Specify functional and non-functional requirements for the users and modules in scope.</li>
                <li>Design the system architecture, key user interactions, data flows and relational database.</li>
                <li>Define implementation workflows for accounts, patients, doctors, appointments, medical records and billing.</li>
                <li>Develop a test plan that checks core functions, validation, access control and usability.</li>
                <li>Identify limitations and practical steps required before a production deployment.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="scope">
            <h3>1.7 Scope of the Project</h3>
            <div class="two-column">
                <div><h4>Included in the design</h4><ul><li>Role-based staff accounts and controlled access.</li><li>Patient demographic and contact profiles.</li><li>Doctor profiles and specialties.</li><li>Appointment creation, status and scheduling.</li><li>Consultation notes and medical-record references.</li><li>Invoices, line items and payment status.</li><li>Basic audit history and administrative reporting.</li></ul></div>
                <div><h4>Outside this student-project scope</h4><ul><li>Clinical decision support, diagnosis recommendations or e-prescribing.</li><li>Laboratory, pharmacy, insurance and national health-record integrations.</li><li>Emergency dispatch, inpatient bed management and medical-device connections.</li><li>Real card processing, insurance claims or financial reconciliation.</li><li>Production hosting, legal certification and real patient-data migration.</li></ul></div>
            </div>
            <p>Deployment to a real healthcare environment would require requirements validation with the institution, applicable privacy and health-record compliance review, threat modelling, security testing, operational support and clinical-safety review.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="significance">
            <h3>1.8 Significance of the Project</h3>
            <p><strong>Patients:</strong> A coordinated registration and appointment workflow can reduce repeated requests for the same basic information and support clearer visit administration.</p>
            <p><strong>Hospital staff:</strong> Structured records and schedules can reduce time spent searching across files and help staff coordinate routine hand-offs.</p>
            <p><strong>Management:</strong> Consistent operational records can support basic activity summaries and help identify workflow bottlenecks.</p>
            <p><strong>Students:</strong> The project provides practical experience in requirements analysis, relational data design, web application architecture, privacy-aware development and software testing.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="chapter-banner" id="chapter-two"><span>CHAPTER TWO</span><h2>System Analysis</h2><p>Existing processes, proposed solution and system requirements.</p></section>
        <section class="report-section" id="existing-system">
            <h3>2.1 Existing System</h3>
            <p>For analysis purposes, the existing workflow is assumed to be primarily paper-based or split across independent office tools. A receptionist records patient details, staff maintain appointment lists, clinicians document visits in files and billing personnel prepare charges separately. These processes vary between institutions; this description is a project assumption to be confirmed through interviews and observation before implementation.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="existing-problems">
            <h3>2.2 Problems of the Existing System</h3>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Operational effect</th><th>Proposed response</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><td>Information in separate locations</td><td>Staff spend time searching and reconciling records.</td><td>Use a shared, role-restricted application and linked records.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Repeated manual data entry</td><td>Duplicate or inconsistent patient and visit details.</td><td>Validate required fields and reuse authorized records.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Paper appointment lists</td><td>Conflicting bookings and difficult schedule updates.</td><td>Check doctor, time and status when scheduling.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Limited access control</td><td>Sensitive information may be visible beyond a user's duties.</td><td>Apply least-privilege access and audit sensitive actions.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Manual billing records</td><td>Payment status and outstanding balances are harder to reconcile.</td><td>Link invoices and payment entries with explicit statuses.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Physical record dependency</td><td>Files can be delayed, damaged or misplaced.</td><td>Use controlled backups and a documented recovery process.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="proposed-system">
            <h3>2.3 Proposed System</h3>
            <p>The proposed solution is a modular web application with a browser-based interface, an ASP.NET Core MVC application layer and a PostgreSQL relational database. Users sign in with individual accounts. The application checks their roles before allowing access to relevant features. Patient, appointment, clinical and billing data are linked through validated identifiers rather than duplicated in unrelated files.</p>
            <p>The design supports basic create, view, update and archive workflows. It uses validation, audit events, encrypted network connections, backups and documented retention policies as necessary controls. For a real deployment, the hospital must define the applicable policies, retention periods, access roles and recovery targets.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="functional-requirements">
            <h3>2.4 Functional Requirements</h3>
            <p>Requirements use “shall” to describe expected system behaviour. They are proposed requirements, not verified production capabilities.</p>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Primary role</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><td>FR-01</td><td>The system shall allow an authorized staff member to sign in and end a session securely.</td><td>All staff</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-02</td><td>An administrator shall create, disable and assign roles to staff accounts.</td><td>Administrator</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-03</td><td>Authorized staff shall register, search, view and update patient demographic details.</td><td>Receptionist</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-04</td><td>An administrator shall maintain doctor profiles, specialties and availability.</td><td>Administrator</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-05</td><td>Authorized staff shall create, reschedule, cancel and confirm appointments.</td><td>Receptionist, doctor</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-06</td><td>A doctor shall view assigned appointments and create a consultation record for a visit.</td><td>Doctor</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-07</td><td>The system shall restrict medical-record access to authorized roles and retain an access history.</td><td>Doctor, authorized staff</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-08</td><td>Billing staff shall create an invoice, record a payment and review the balance.</td><td>Billing staff</td><td>Must</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-09</td><td>Authorized users shall view operational summaries appropriate to their role.</td><td>Administrator</td><td>Should</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FR-10</td><td>The system shall retain a timestamped audit event for sensitive changes.</td><td>Administrator</td><td>Must</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="nonfunctional-requirements">
            <h3>2.5 Non-Functional Requirements</h3>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>Requirement / acceptance measure</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><td>Security</td><td>Require authenticated access to protected functions, enforce server-side authorization on every request, hash passwords with a reviewed identity framework and use HTTPS in deployment.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Privacy</td><td>Collect only necessary information, limit access by role, avoid sensitive data in logs and define retention and deletion procedures with the institution.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Usability</td><td>Use consistent navigation, readable labels, helpful validation and keyboard-accessible controls; assess with representative staff.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Performance</td><td>Set a measurable response-time target with stakeholders; as a preliminary test target, common pages should respond within two seconds under the agreed test load.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Use transactions for related changes, monitor failures and verify that backups can be restored against an agreed recovery objective.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Maintainability</td><td>Separate presentation, application logic and persistence; apply migrations and automated tests to schema changes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Compatibility</td><td>Support current versions of the institution's approved desktop browsers and responsive layouts for common screen sizes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>Use semantic headings, visible focus, sufficient contrast, form labels and text alternatives for meaningful diagrams.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
            <p>Performance figures are proposed student-project targets. They must be measured and agreed with users; they are not results claimed by this report.</p>
        </section>
