        <section class="chapter-banner" id="chapter-four"><span>CHAPTER FOUR</span><h2>System Implementation</h2><p>Proposed technology stack, data implementation and module workflows.</p></section>
        <section class="report-section" id="technologies">
            <h3>4.1 Technologies Used</h3>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Technology</th><th>Role in the proposed implementation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><td>ASP.NET Core MVC</td><td>HTTP routing, controllers, Razor views, model binding and server-side validation.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Entity Framework Core</td><td>Typed data access, entity mapping, transactions and database schema migrations.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>PostgreSQL / Npgsql</td><td>Relational persistence and the .NET database provider.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Bootstrap and custom CSS</td><td>Responsive layout components and consistent interface styling.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>ASP.NET Core Identity (recommended)</td><td>Account management, password hashing, sign-in, lockout and role authorization.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Automated test framework</td><td>Unit and integration tests for workflows, persistence and access policies.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
            <p>The application uses ASP.NET Core MVC targeting .NET 10, Entity Framework Core and the Npgsql PostgreSQL provider. Its migration retains the original Students example table and adds the HMS tables for staff, patients, doctors, appointments, medical records, invoices, payments and audit events. Migrations are not run automatically: review the generated migration, back up any existing database and apply it explicitly to the intended development database.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="languages">
            <h3>4.2 Programming Languages</h3>
            <ul><li><strong>C#:</strong> server-side request handling, domain rules, validation and persistence logic.</li><li><strong>HTML:</strong> semantic structure for pages, tables, navigation and forms.</li><li><strong>CSS:</strong> visual design, responsive layout, print styling and accessible focus states.</li><li><strong>JavaScript:</strong> optional progressive enhancements such as confirmation prompts; important validation and access checks remain server-side.</li><li><strong>SQL:</strong> database definition, constraints, indexes and diagnostic queries when needed.</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="database-implementation">
            <h3>4.3 Database Implementation</h3>
            <p>Database implementation should proceed through version-controlled Entity Framework migrations. Define entities and relationships, review generated SQL, apply schema changes to a development database, and verify constraints with automated tests before deploying. Production credentials should be supplied through a managed secret store or environment-specific secret configuration and must never be committed to source control.</p>
            <ol class="numbered-list"><li>Create the schema from the approved ERD and add foreign-key, uniqueness and check constraints.</li><li>Seed only non-sensitive reference data, such as permitted role names; never seed real patients or passwords.</li><li>Use parameterized queries or the ORM's parameter handling; do not build SQL from user-provided strings.</li><li>Encrypt connections and backups, restrict database network access and apply least privilege to database accounts.</li><li>Test migration rollback and backup restoration in a non-production environment.</li></ol>
            <div class="callout"><strong>Current implementation status</strong><span>The application code implements staff authentication, first-admin setup, role-restricted workflows and PostgreSQL entities. Apply the migration and configure the database before use. Real-world deployment still requires institution-specific privacy, security, clinical-safety and operational approval.</span></div>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="system-modules">
            <h3>4.4 System Modules</h3>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Main responsibility</th><th>Access</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><td>Authentication and administration</td><td>Sign-in, staff account lifecycle, roles and audit review.</td><td>All staff / administrator tasks</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Patient management</td><td>Demographic profile, search and status management.</td><td>Reception and authorized clinical roles</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Doctor management</td><td>Staff-linked specialty, availability and active status.</td><td>Administrator; limited directory view to staff</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Appointment management</td><td>Booking, rescheduling, cancellation and visit status.</td><td>Reception and assigned doctor</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Medical records</td><td>Consultation notes with authorship and access history.</td><td>Authorized clinical roles</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Billing and payments</td><td>Invoices, charge items, payment entries and balances.</td><td>Billing staff; limited patient-facing summary if approved</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="login">
            <h3>4.5 Login and Registration</h3>
            <p>Staff accounts should be created or invited by an authorized administrator rather than through an unrestricted public sign-up page. Sign-in should use the institution-approved identity provider or ASP.NET Core Identity. Passwords must be processed by a vetted password-hashing implementation; they must never be stored or logged in plaintext. Enforce authorization on the server for every protected operation, apply session-expiration and lockout controls, and provide a safe account-recovery process.</p>
            <p>Registration should collect only staff information needed for administration. Role changes and account deactivation should be auditable. Multi-factor authentication is recommended for privileged users and should be required where institutional policy mandates it.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="patient-management">
            <h3>4.6 Patient Management</h3>
            <p>Authorized staff can create a patient profile after checking for a likely existing record, reducing accidental duplicates. The form validates required fields and formats, explains why information is collected and avoids requesting unnecessary sensitive details. Search results should expose only enough information to select the correct patient. Corrections and status changes should be recorded with the responsible user's identifier and timestamp.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="doctor-management">
            <h3>4.7 Doctor Management</h3>
            <p>An administrator links a doctor profile to an authenticated staff account and maintains the doctor's specialty, approved professional reference and schedule availability. Changes should be validated and audited. An inactive doctor should not receive new appointments, while historical visits remain associated with the correct doctor record.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="appointment-management">
            <h3>4.8 Appointment Management</h3>
            <p>When an appointment is requested, the system verifies that the patient and doctor are active, the requested time meets scheduling rules and the doctor has no conflicting active appointment. A transaction or database-level conflict strategy should prevent simultaneous requests from booking the same slot. Rescheduling and cancellation preserve the prior status in the audit history. Notifications are optional and require institution-approved channels and message content.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="medical-records">
            <h3>4.9 Medical Records</h3>
            <p>A doctor records information relevant to a consultation, links it to the patient and appointment, and saves it with author and timestamp metadata. Medical notes are sensitive: access must be checked on the server, views should avoid unnecessary exposure, and audit events should capture access or change without copying the full clinical text into logs. Corrections should preserve provenance according to clinical policy rather than silently overwriting history.</p>
            <p>Templates and fields must be reviewed by clinical stakeholders. The student project does not define a diagnosis standard, clinical coding system or legally compliant record-retention schedule.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="billing">
            <h3>4.10 Billing and Payments</h3>
            <p>Billing staff prepare an invoice from approved charge items, review the calculated total and record payment status. The application should use decimal currency types, validate non-negative amounts, preserve issued invoice history and calculate balances consistently. Reversals or corrections should be represented by auditable entries rather than hidden edits.</p>
            <p>This design records payment metadata only; it does not collect or store card numbers, security codes or online banking credentials. A real payment gateway requires a separately approved integration, security assessment and reconciliation process. Amounts, taxes, currency and financial permissions must follow local policy.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="chapter-banner" id="chapter-five"><span>CHAPTER FIVE</span><h2>Testing and Conclusion</h2><p>Verification approach, expected outcomes and future work.</p></section>
        <section class="report-section" id="system-testing">
            <h3>5.1 System Testing</h3>
            <p>Testing should use synthetic data in an isolated environment. The following cases form an initial acceptance plan. Results must be entered after execution; they are not pre-filled as passed.</p>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Test ID</th><th>Test case</th><th>Expected result</th><th>Execution status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                <tr><td>TC-01</td><td>Sign in with a valid active staff account.</td><td>Authorized dashboard appears; session is established securely.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-02</td><td>Sign in with an invalid password.</td><td>Access is denied with a generic message; sensitive details are not disclosed.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-03</td><td>Open an administrator-only action as a receptionist.</td><td>Server denies access and records the relevant security event.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-04</td><td>Create a patient with missing required fields.</td><td>Record is not saved; clear field validation is shown.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-05</td><td>Book two active appointments for one doctor at the same time.</td><td>Conflicting booking is prevented, including concurrent submissions.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-06</td><td>Request a medical record without the required role.</td><td>Record content is not returned; the access attempt is handled safely.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-07</td><td>Issue an invoice with multiple line items.</td><td>Total and balance are correct and transaction remains consistent.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-08</td><td>Submit an invalid or negative payment amount.</td><td>Input is rejected; stored invoice balance remains unchanged.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-09</td><td>Restore a database backup in a test environment.</td><td>Records and relationships are restored within agreed recovery targets.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
                <tr><td>TC-10</td><td>Use key pages with keyboard-only navigation and a narrow viewport.</td><td>Controls remain accessible and layouts remain readable.</td><td>Not run</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
            <p>Unit tests cover domain rules and calculations. Integration tests use an isolated test database to check persistence, constraints and authorization. Acceptance testing should involve representative users and approved test scenarios. Security and recovery tests should be reviewed by qualified personnel before any operational deployment.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="results">
            <h3>5.2 Results</h3>
            <p>This deliverable includes the academic report and working application code for sign-in, role-based navigation, patient/doctor management, appointments, medical records, billing, staff administration and password changes. The HMS schema migration has been applied to the configured PostgreSQL database. The application builds successfully and the database CRUD smoke test passed: temporary patient, doctor, appointment, clinical-record, invoice and payment records were created, read, searched, updated and deleted inside a transaction, then rolled back. The test does not claim production readiness, clinical validation or completion of manual user-acceptance testing.</p>
            <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Deliverable</th><th>Outcome in this report</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Requirements and scope</td><td>Documented for the student-project system.</td></tr><tr><td>System and database design</td><td>Documented with diagrams and schema tables.</td></tr><tr><td>Application modules</td><td>Implemented in the web project with the PostgreSQL schema applied.</td></tr><tr><td>Build and route smoke checks</td><td>Build succeeded; setup/login and report pages responded in the local browser.</td></tr><tr><td>Database CRUD smoke test</td><td>Passed against PostgreSQL; temporary test rows were rolled back.</td></tr><tr><td>Manual user acceptance tests</td><td>Not run with a hospital user or real workflow.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="advantages">
            <h3>5.3 Advantages of the System</h3>
            <ul><li>Centralized and consistently structured information for the included workflows.</li><li>Reduced repeated entry and easier retrieval of authorized records.</li><li>Clear appointment status and doctor schedule workflows.</li><li>Role-based access design for sensitive administrative and clinical functions.</li><li>Linked invoices and payment records that support balance review.</li><li>Modular architecture that can be tested and extended over time.</li><li>Potential to support operational reporting when the data is complete and appropriately governed.</li></ul>
            <p>These are expected benefits of a properly implemented and adopted system, not measured outcomes from a hospital deployment.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="limitations">
            <h3>5.4 Limitations</h3>
            <ul><li>The HMS migration must be reviewed and applied to the intended PostgreSQL database before the workflows can store and retrieve data.</li><li>This report's assumptions about existing hospital processes need validation through stakeholder research.</li><li>The system does not integrate with clinical coding, laboratories, pharmacy, insurance, national health records or payment gateways.</li><li>The design does not cover every hospital department, clinical standard, integration or jurisdictional requirement.</li><li>Performance, accessibility, security, backup and usability targets have not been independently measured in a production environment.</li><li>Production controls such as multifactor authentication, formal account recovery, monitoring, restore-tested backups and institution-approved retention policies require further work.</li><li>A web system depends on reliable power, network, hosting, support staff and staff training. Digitization alone does not guarantee accurate data or improved clinical outcomes.</li></ul>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="recommendations">
            <h3>5.5 Recommendations</h3>
            <ol class="numbered-list"><li>Interview intended users and validate workflows, terminology, roles and requirements before coding the clinical or financial modules.</li><li>Replace the student example schema with reviewed hospital entities and migrations; use synthetic data throughout development.</li><li>Implement a maintained identity framework, server-side authorization, audit controls, input validation and secure secret management.</li><li>Obtain privacy, information-security, clinical-safety and legal review appropriate to the deployment jurisdiction before handling real patient information.</li><li>Conduct usability and accessibility testing with representative users and address findings before rollout.</li><li>Define hosting, monitoring, incident response, backup, restore, retention and business-continuity procedures.</li><li>Integrate external systems and payment services only through approved interfaces and after separate technical and security reviews.</li><li>Train users, deploy in a controlled pilot, evaluate measurable outcomes and improve the system from evidence.</li></ol>
        </section>
        <section class="report-section" id="conclusion">
            <h3>5.6 Conclusion</h3>
            <p>This project has presented the analysis and design of a Hospital Management System for selected administrative and clinical workflows. It explains the need, objectives and boundaries; describes existing-process challenges and requirements; and documents a layered architecture, use cases, data flow, entity relationships and database design. It also outlines proposed implementation workflows and a test plan.</p>
            <p>A thoughtfully implemented HMS could help staff coordinate records and routine work, provided it is designed with the people who will use it and governed to protect sensitive information. The current deliverable is an academic design report, not a production-ready clinical application. Completing the software, validating the requirements, executing the test plan and obtaining appropriate institutional approvals are necessary next steps before operational use.</p>
        </section>

